        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A pressure washer should never run without water. The pump depends on that water for lubrication and cooling from the very first stroke, and a dry start can damage seals in ways that only show up later as leaks, weak pressure or pulsing. Connect the supply, open the faucet fully and purge the air through the gun every time before you switch on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            With water connected, the clock to watch is bypass time. On machines that keep running when you release the trigger, which includes most gas units, many manuals set a limit of about two minutes. A thermal relief valve, if fitted, softens the damage but does not make long idling safe. Check your own manual for the exact figure, and when you stop spraying for more than a moment, switch the machine off.
          </p>
